WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
Assessment begins by identifying data that must remain confidential for a long time.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:18.100
Teams should look for regulated records, health information, legal material, government data, sensitive personal information, authentication secrets, strategic business information, research and development data, and long-term archives.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.440
Then they should map the systems that transmit, store, replicate, and back up that data.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.580
Without that mapping, harvest-now, decrypt-later stays abstract.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.760
The next step is reviewing cryptographic dependencies.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.220
Which TLS endpoints protect the data?

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:41.840
Which VPNs, SSH services, secure email systems, storage products, cloud services, APIs, and partner integrations are involved?

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:49.180
Which libraries, certificates, key management systems, hardware security modules, and vendor products do those systems rely on?

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:55.480
The answer often spans multiple teams and ownership boundaries, which is why this work should not live only in a security spreadsheet.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:05.620
Prioritization should consider sensitivity, confidentiality lifetime, volume, external exposure, mission importance, dependency complexity, and migration difficulty.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:13.280
A public API carrying sensitive records for long-term retention may deserve earlier attention than an internal test endpoint with synthetic data.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.020
A difficult-to-migrate legacy system may also deserve early planning because lead time is part of risk.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.280
The highest priority is often where value, exposure, and slow migration overlap.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:32.580
Risk assessment should involve security, engineering, legal, privacy, compliance, operations, and business stakeholders.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.000
Legal and privacy teams can explain data obligations.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.860
Engineering teams can identify real dependencies.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.080
Operations teams can describe change windows and reliability constraints.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.380
Business owners can clarify impact.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:52.480
Together, they can separate theoretical risk from actionable risk and build a migration roadmap that is realistic enough to execute.
